# Private config source pointer (git-ignored).
#
# The public repo ships only committed templates for the operational data
# (etc/machines.ini, etc/team.ini). The real values live in a separate,
# access-controlled private repository — a small git repo whose tracked files
# are `machines.ini` and `team.ini` (mirroring the consumer's etc/). This
# file tells `fetch-private-data` where to find a checkout of that repo.
#
# Copy this file to `.private-source` (untracked, machine-specific) and set
# exactly one retrieval mechanism:
#
#   1. A local checkout of the private repo:
PRIVATE_DATA_SOURCE=/srv/private-config
#
#   2. A git URL (+ optional ref); fetched on demand into var/private-data
#      (git-ignored, like the rest of /var/):
# PRIVATE_DATA_GIT=git@example.com:team/app-private-config.git
# PRIVATE_DATA_REF=main
#
#   When neither mechanism is set, the private files are expected to already
#   be present directly in etc/ (regular files, e.g. shipped by the private
#   repo's git-archive deploy); fetch-private-data then only validates them
#   (warn-only, never aborts).
#
# This file never discloses the private repo's identity in the public history
# (it is git-ignored). Access control on the private repo is the real security
# boundary — see doc/system/private-config.md.
